<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boxer Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1899–1901</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Boxer Uprising, Yihetuan Movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Boxer Rebellion was an anti-foreign and anti-Christian uprising in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> led by the Yihetuan, the 'Militia United in Righteousness', known in the West as the Boxers for their martial rituals. Spreading through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and Zhili in 1899 and 1900, the Boxers killed missionaries and thousands of Chinese Christians, and in June 1900, after the Qing court under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> declared war on the foreign powers, they besieged the foreign legations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. An international force of eight nations relieved the legations in August 1900 and occupied the capital, and the Boxer Protocol of 1901 imposed a huge indemnity on China.</p></div>
